(vector (map (:adr/authoritative_for (vector "kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn の実UDPリレーリスナー（kotoba.turn.listener）の設計根拠" "このADRシリーズにおける turn の「実際に稼働可能」到達点の記録")), (:adr/supersedes (vector)), (:adr/superseded_by (vector)), (:adr/topic "telecom-independent-substrate-dtn-rcs-mesh-satellite"), (:adr/related (vector "90-docs/adr/2607162020-kotoba-lang-turn-allocation-state-machine.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607162033-kotoba-lang-wire-shared-transport-dtn-refactor.edn" "orgs/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:adr/id "adr-2607162110-kotoba-lang-turn-real-udp-relay-listener"), (:adr/doc_type :adr), (:adr/last_verified "2026-07-16"), (:adr/body "---\nid: adr-2607162110-kotoba-lang-turn-real-udp-relay-listener\ntitle: \"ADR-2607162110: kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn gains a real UDP relay listener (Phase 3b) — the repo is now an actually-running TURN relay\"\nstatus: accepted\ndoc_type: adr\ntopic: telecom-independent-substrate-dtn-rcs-mesh-satellite\nauthoritative: true\nlast_verified: 2026-07-16\nauthoritative_for:\n  - kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn の実UDPリレーリスナー（kotoba.turn.listener）の設計根拠\n  - このADRシリーズにおける turn の「実際に稼働可能」到達点の記録\nrelated:\n  - 90-docs/adr/2607162020-kotoba-lang-turn-allocation-state-machine.edn\n  - 90-docs/adr/2607162033-kotoba-lang-wire-shared-transport-dtn-refactor.edn\n  - orgs/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn\nsupersedes: []\nsuperseded_by: []\n---\n\n# ADR-2607162110: kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn gains a real UDP relay listener (Phase 3b) — the repo is now an actually-running TURN relay\n\n**Status**: accepted\n**Date**: 2026-07-16\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki\n\n## Problem\n\nPhase 3a (ADR-2607162020) built the pure allocation/permission/channel-\nbinding state machine and ChannelData/demux codecs — deliberately zero\nsocket I/O, matching this repo's founding ADR's explicit \"message/state\nlayer first, listener is a separate later phase\" plan. This ADR is that\nlistener: the piece that turns `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn` from a protocol\nlibrary into something that actually relays data between a real client and\na real peer over the network — the same \"close the actually-runs gap\"\nstandard this whole ADR series has applied to `kotoba-lang/dtn`.\n\n## Decision\n\n### Decision 1: `kotoba.turn.listener` — real `node:dgram` sockets wiring every existing pure namespace together\n\nOne main UDP listening socket per server, one additional ephemeral-port\nrelay-side UDP socket **per allocation** (opened at Allocate time, closed\nat expiry/deletion) — matching real TURN semantics, where the \"relayed\naddress\" a client receives IS that dedicated socket's own address. Inbound\ndatagrams are classified via `kotoba.turn.demux/classify-datagram` (Phase\n3a) and dispatched to STUN request/indication handling\n(`kotoba.turn.stun` + `kotoba.turn.credential` + `kotoba.turn.allocation`,\nall pre-existing) or ChannelData relay (`kotoba.turn.channeldata`,\npre-existing). No new protocol logic was invented in this ADR — every\npiece of state/codec logic it calls already existed and was already\ntested; this ADR's own new code is purely the socket wiring and dispatch.\n\n### Decision 2: both relay directions are real, and both are proven, not just one\n\nA TURN relay's value is the FULL path — client→relay→peer AND\npeer→relay→client. This ADR's listener implements and the demo proves\nboth explicitly: a peer's real reply is picked up by the allocation's own\nrelay-side socket and forwarded back to the original client as a STUN Data\nindication (the general path) or, when a channel is bound, as lighter\nChannelData framing (the fast path) — verified as genuinely DIFFERENT wire\nformats reaching the client correctly in each case (scenario 2 checks the\nreply classifies as `:channel-data`, not a STUN Data indication, proving\nthe fast path is actually taken, not just that data arrived).\n\n### Decision 3: only Allocate is cryptographically verified — a narrower, disclosed authentication surface than production TURN\n\nRefresh/CreatePermission/ChannelBind trust \"does a live allocation already\nexist for this client address/port\" rather than re-verifying\nMESSAGE-INTEGRITY on every subsequent request. This is narrower than a\nproduction coturn-class server (which typically re-authenticates every\nrequest) and is recorded here explicitly as a real, disclosed scope\nnarrowing — not a silently-assumed equivalence. The security-relevant\ngate — can an unauthenticated party get anything relayed at all — is what\nscenario 3 actually proves closed (a forged-secret Allocate creates no\nallocation and causes no relay side effect); tightening the follow-up\nrequests' authentication to match production TURN is additive future work,\nnot required for this ADR's own claim.\n\n### Decision 4: two real bugs found and fixed during implementation, recorded rather than silently absorbed\n\n1. Relay sockets initially bound with no explicit host, so Node defaulted\n   to `0.0.0.0` — a literally non-functional `XOR-RELAYED-ADDRESS`\n   (`0.0.0.0:port` is not a usable return address). Fixed with a\n   `:relay-host` option (default `127.0.0.1`, documented as needing\n   override to a real public/routable address for actual deployment — this\n   listener is correct in-repo/local-network shape, not yet a\n   production-deployment default).\n2. An async-ordering bug in the demo itself (not the listener): a\n   `promesa.core/p/let` binding that tries to \"arm a listener for a future\n   message\" inside the same `p/let` as the triggering send deadlocks,\n   because `p/let` eagerly awaits every binding in order — the listener\n   promise blocks before the triggering send ever runs. Fixed by creating\n   such promises via a plain `let` before the triggering send, awaited in\n   a later `p/let` binding. Recorded because it's a real, non-obvious\n   ClojureScript/promesa pitfall this workspace's future nbb-based demos\n   (there will be more, per the runtime-priority rules) should know about.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `nbb --classpath \"src:test:../bytes/src\" test/kotoba/turn/listener_demo.cljs` → `RESULT: 3/3 scenarios passed`, exit 0. Independently re-run from a cold clone by the orchestrating session — output identical to the implementing agent's report, including exact STUN message-type numbers (`259`/`264`/`265`/`23`/`275`) and both relay directions genuinely observed. Scenario 1: authenticated Allocate + CreatePermission + Send-indication relay, both hops real. Scenario 2: ChannelBind fast path, both hops real, reply genuinely classified as ChannelData not a Data indication. Scenario 3: forged-credential Allocate rejected with an ERROR-CODE response, no allocation created, no relay side effect for a subsequent Send indication from the rejected party.\n- `clojure -M:test` → still 41 tests / 94 assertions, 0 failures (unaffected — `.cljs`-only file, independently re-confirmed). `clojure -M:lint` → 0 errors, 0 warnings (independently re-confirmed).\n- Pushed to `github.com/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn` (public), commit `b03d1bf38fc714c0ca459f7499e08e7a18b08d5c`.\n- `manifest/west.yml`: pin advanced via `--entry org-ietf-turn` (1-line diff, `d8541b405901→b03d1bf38fc7`, `verify-west-pins: 1 件の pin 変更をすべて検証 OK`), landed via an isolated sibling-path worktree + GitHub API server-side merge, matching every prior ADR in this series.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn` is now a genuinely running TURN relay for its\n  UDP client-facing path — the \"not yet a runnable TURN relay server\" line\n  from the founding ADR's own Consequences section is closed for that\n  transport (TCP/TLS client-facing remains unimplemented, named, not\n  claimed).\n- The net/turn/dtn shared-lib consolidation plan (Phase 1 → 2 → 3a → 3b)\n  has now delivered two independently-verified, genuinely-operational\n  network services (`kotoba-lang/dtn`'s delay-tolerant messaging substrate,\n  `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn`'s relay) sharing a common foundation\n  (`kotoba-lang/bytes`) — the dependency-efficiency goal is demonstrated at\n  the scale of two full services now, not just shared utility functions.\n- Decision 3's narrower per-request authentication surface is a real,\n  disclosed limitation for anyone considering this for a security-sensitive\n  deployment — future work, not silently equivalent to production TURN.\n- Long-term credential mechanism, IPv6, DoS/quota limits, exact\n  RFC-specified error-code attribute payload details beyond a minimal\n  ERROR-CODE, and TCP/TLS client-facing transport all remain unimplemented\n  — the same gaps the founding ADR and Phase 3a already named, confirmed\n  still open, not newly claimed closed.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- **Re-verifying MESSAGE-INTEGRITY on every request type (Refresh/\n  CreatePermission/ChannelBind), not just Allocate.** Deferred rather than\n  rejected outright: the implementing agent's report flagged this\n  explicitly as a scope-narrowing choice; this ADR records it rather than\n  silently expanding scope beyond what was built and verified. A follow-up\n  ADR can close it if warranted.\n- **A full RFC-exact STUN ERROR-CODE attribute encoding** (reason-phrase\n  text, exact error-class/number split) for every rejection path. Rejected\n  for this pass in favor of a minimal, working error response — matching\n  Phase 3a's own \"honestly-scoped, don't over-engineer beyond what's\n  verified\" discipline; `stun.cljc` only defines one error-type constant\n  today, extending its error-response vocabulary is separable future work.\n- **Deploying this listener anywhere** (binding to a real public address,\n  running it as a long-lived process). Out of scope for this ADR\n  entirely — this is a library/demo-level implementation proven correct in\n  a local/loopback shape; actual deployment (key management, public\n  addressing, operational monitoring) is explicitly a different org's\n  concern per this workspace's own \"protocol implementation vs. deployment\n  operations are different axes\" convention (see `kotoba-lang/webrtc`'s\n  ADR addendum for the precedent).\n\n## References\n\n- `90-docs/adr/2607162020-kotoba-lang-turn-allocation-state-machine.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2607162033-kotoba-lang-wire-shared-transport-dtn-refactor.edn`\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn/README.edn` — https://github.com/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn/src/kotoba/turn/listener.cljs`\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn/test/kotoba/turn/listener_demo.cljs`\n"), (:adr/artifacts "{:kotoba-lang-org-ietf-turn \"kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn @ b03d1bf38fc7 (pushed, public)\", :manifest \"orgs/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn pin advanced via --entry org-ietf-turn, pin-verified\"}"), (:db/id -1), (:adr/authoritative true), (:adr/title "ADR-2607162110: kotoba-lang/org-ietf-turn gains a real UDP relay listener (Phase 3b) — the repo is now an actually-running TURN relay"), (:adr/source-format "edn")))